                        Here in the Antarctic, the emperor penguin's breeding season has just ended. 
The eggs have been laid. 
And the female emperor penguins are famished from months of not being able to hunt. It is now the male partners' turn to keep the eggs warm until the eggs hatch and the females return. With the temperatures dropping as low as -55 °C, the transferring of the egg has to be quick, as even a few minutes on the icy floors is enough to freeze the eggs. With the eggs successfully transferred, it is now the male penguin's job to keep the egg warm and toasty for the coming few months while the females go and hunt. The eggs are kept warm under the belly fat and floof of the male. The egg is also perched on top of the male's feet to keep them off the ground. And here we are following the penguin, Gino. Though not the biggest of the pack, he managed to woo a female penguin during the courting season and is now guarding their egg, incubating it alongside many other incubating males. With temperatures dropping and blizzards coming unannounced, the males are staying huddled up in groups to cut back on the heat lost in front of the blizzards that can reach speeds of up to 200 kmph. 
Today it is Gino's turn to stay on the outside of the pack. The males rotate every so often on who stays on the inside of the pack. The number of penguins who returned to breed this season has decreased from last year due to the melting glaciers. When adults return to start a new mating cycle in March or April, delayed freezing means their traditional coastal ice platforms may not exist yet. Unlike smaller penguins, emperors are too heavy and clumsy to easily climb steep rocky cliffs or tall glacial ice shelves. Meanwhile, juvenile emperor penguins such as the one-year-old Pingu have already entered the waters. They will not be returning to the mating site for the next 3-6 years based on their growth. However, poor nutrition during their ocean years slows growth. Juveniles that struggle to gain weight may delay their first return to the breeding colony past the typical 3-to-6-year mark. 
Young penguins spend their first years at sea learning to hunt krill, fish, and squid. As data notes, algae and plankton grow on the underside of sea ice. When ice melts, krill populations drop, making it much harder for inexperienced 1-year-olds to find enough food to survive.<br>

