<p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
The survival of these chicks had certainly become harder. As tho they already didn't have the risks of predators, starving, and freezing working against them, they now have to survive the ice as well and not in the way you might think. The chicks need sea ice to remain solid until December or January so they can replace their fuzzy down with waterproof feathers. Satellite studies by the British Antarctic Survey showed that early ice breakup in the Bellingshausen Sea wiped out all chicks across four out of five colonies. Without waterproof coats, chicks freeze or drown when the ice beneath them shatters. And with the odds ever stacking against them, we can both just pray that nature performs a miracle and the ice freezes over once again.
